#![enable(implicit_some)]
// The "to code" rules for runs (pipes, hoses, tubes and cables), and the PROFILES that group
// them: what a run laid in a home, aboard a ship or in a spacecraft must keep to (2026-11-02,
// spline routing rung 0, docs/design/spline-routing.md sections 7.1 and 18). Read by
// src/ship/run_rules.rs (disk first, the copy built into the exe if this file is missing or does
// not parse). Nothing evaluates these rows yet: rung 8 does (`ship::run_checks`), each kind by
// the run's computed curve, its anchors and its neighbours in the same cells.
//
// UNITS. Every length is in METRES (fields ending `_m`), every angle in DEGREES (`_deg`), a slope
// as a rise over its run (a ratio: 0.0208 is 1/4 inch per foot), a force in newtons (`_n`), a
// temperature in degrees Celsius (`_c`), a pipe size by its DN (the metric nominal size, ISO 6708:
// DN15 is 1/2 inch). Where the source is imperial, `as_read` keeps its own words and units, so a
// reader can match the row to the finding.
//
// SOURCES. Every row names where its numbers come from: R1 to R44 are the findings of
// docs/reference/findings/2026-11-01-pipe-and-cable-routing-rules.md, D1 to D19 those of
// 2026-11-01-manual-door-releases.md, and Ours("why") marks a number the project chose, with its
// reason. A test (src/ship/run_rules.rs) reads the findings documents' numbered paragraphs and
// fails on a row with no source and on one naming a finding that does not exist.
//
// STANDING says how binding the source is WHERE THE PROFILE APPLIES, so the editor's words never
// call a row "code" that is not law there (design 7.1):
//   Law            a regulation that binds there (24 CFR 3280 in a manufactured home, 46 CFR aboard a US vessel)
//   Standard       a consensus or agency standard the law there incorporates or the work there follows
//                  (IEEE 45.8, which 46 CFR 111.60-5 incorporates; NASA-STD-8739.4A for a NASA spacecraft)
//   Specification  a contract specification, binding only on the projects that adopt it (the DoD's UFGS)
//   Guidance       advice, or a rule for somewhere else used as the closest public rule (an FAA advisory
//                  circular, a design guide, OSHA's workplace rule applied to a home)
//   Maker          a maker's data sheet or installation guide
//   Ours           no public source was read: the project's number, with its reason
//
// FIELD REFERENCE
//   profiles[]   id, name, draws_on (what the profile follows, in words), source.
//   equipment[]  the equipment a rule can name (keep-outs, separations from a flue): id, meaning.
//                Rung 8 matches them to machine rows; a rule naming an undeclared one fails the test.
//   rules[]
//     id         unique
//     kind       one of the design's rule kinds (7.1), by its own word, with that kind's numbers:
//                bend_radius, fittings_only, support_spacing, support_near, separation, keep_out,
//                vertical_order, no_shared, slope, drain_turn, penetration, pull_limit, fill,
//                allowed_material
//     applies    which runs the rule is about (every field optional; an empty list matches any):
//                  materials  material ids of data/piping/pipe_materials.ron
//                  forms      Pipe (any fluid line), Cable, Raceway (a conduit or tray that holds cables)
//                  behaviours Rigid, Bendable, Flexible
//                  contents   what the run carries: Electricity, Water, HotWater, Air, Data, Fuel,
//                             Nutrient, Waste
//                  tags       material tags ALL of which the material must carry (declared in the catalog)
//                  without_tags  tags NONE of which it may carry
//                  dn         a nominal size range, inclusive: (from, to)
//                  od_m       an outside-diameter range, inclusive
//                  awg        a wire gauge range, inclusive (a LARGER gauge number is a THINNER wire)
//                  volts      the cable's voltage rating range, inclusive
//     profiles   the profiles the rule belongs to (dwelling_us, ship_us, spacecraft)
//     standing   above
//     as_read    the source's own numbers and units
//     source     [R(n), D(n), Ours("why")], one or more
//     note       anything a reader needs to apply it
//
// WHAT IS NOT HERE, AND WHY. No rule keeps a cable a set distance from a water pipe: no source read
// gives one (R35), so the game does not invent one and present it as code. The rules that keep
// water off electricity are the keep-outs over panels and switchboards, the vertical order, and
// no shared holes or supports.
(
    profiles: [
        (
            id: "dwelling_us",
            name: "A home, United States",
            draws_on: "A home's own wiring and plumbing: 24 CFR 3280 (the federal manufactured home standard, public law) where it has a number; the DoD's UFGS for metal pipe, conduit and data cabling; the makers' radii; OSHA's workplace rules where they are the public mirror of a National Electrical Code rule nobody here has read (R42).",
            source: [R(8), R(9), R(13), R(42)],
        ),
        (
            id: "ship_us",
            name: "A ship's services, United States",
            draws_on: "A ship's own services (corridors, machinery spaces, the hull): 46 CFR, and IEEE 45.8-2016, which 46 CFR 111.60-5 incorporates for cable. Where 46 CFR names a standard that is sold and was not read (MSS SP-58 for pipe hangers, R44), the closest public table is used and says so.",
            source: [R(5), R(14), R(29), R(39), R(40)],
        ),
        (
            id: "spacecraft",
            name: "A spacecraft",
            draws_on: "NASA-STD-8739.4A for wiring harnesses, with the FAA's AC 43.13-1B (aircraft wiring) as the analogue where NASA gives no number.",
            source: [R(16), R(17)],
        ),
    ],
    equipment: [
        (id: "electrical_panel", meaning: "A panelboard or breaker panel (a home's consumer unit)."),
        (id: "switchboard", meaning: "A ship's switchboard (46 CFR 111.30)."),
        (id: "flue", meaning: "A flue or vent carrying combustion gases away from a burner."),
        (id: "water_heater_flue", meaning: "The flue of a gas or oil water heater."),
    ],
    rules: [
        // ── bend_radius: the tightest a run may bend ───────────────────────────────────────────
        (
            id: "bend_copper_table",
            kind: bend_radius(installed: SizeRow(Bender)),
            applies: (materials: ["copper_l_annealed", "copper_l_drawn"]),
            profiles: ["dwelling_us", "ship_us"],
            standing: Guidance,
            as_read: "CDA Table 4.1, by size and temper: annealed 3/4 in at 1/4 to 9 in at 1 1/4; drawn 1 3/4 in at 3/8 to 3 in at 3/4",
            source: [R(1), R(5)],
            note: "The radii are the size rows' own (data/piping/pipe_materials.ron), from the Copper Development Association's handbook, an industry handbook rather than law. Aboard a ship 46 CFR 56.80-5 sets no radius (R5): the bender decides, and these are the bender's numbers.",
        ),
        (
            id: "bend_pex",
